<span class="kicker">2026-02-15 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>What Do Landscape Architects Do?</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Landscape architects are licensed professionals who plan, design, and manage outdoor spaces for both aesthetic appeal and functional use. Unlike general landscapers, they work on larger-scale projects such as parks, commercial developments, public spaces, and complex residential landscapes. Their work combines creativity, environmental science, and engineering principles to create sustainable and visually appealing outdoor environments.</p>
<p>A landscape architect’s responsibilities include conducting site analysis, designing layouts for plants, hardscapes, pathways, and water features, and addressing issues such as drainage, erosion control, and climate adaptability. They also consider zoning laws, accessibility, and environmental impact when developing plans. In Colorado, landscape architects must account for local soil conditions, elevation changes, and water efficiency to ensure long-term success.</p>
<p>Beyond design, landscape architects often collaborate with engineers, urban planners, and contractors to oversee project implementation. Their expertise ensures that outdoor spaces are functional, safe, sustainable, and visually harmonious, enhancing property value and community enjoyment.</p>
<h2><b>Landscape Architect Work Environment</b></h2>
<p>The work environment of a landscape architect is a combination of office-based planning and on-site project management. In the office, landscape architects use CAD, 3D modeling, GIS mapping, and other design software to develop detailed plans, create visual presentations for clients, and coordinate with contractors and consultants. Meetings with clients, engineers, and municipal authorities are also a common part of the office workflow.</p>
<p>On-site, landscape architects supervise construction, ensure designs are implemented correctly, and address any unforeseen challenges that arise during installation. They may visit residential yards, commercial properties, public parks, or large-scale infrastructure projects. In Colorado, on-site work requires consideration of local climate, soil conditions, and topography, which can influence plant health and the durability of hardscape features.</p>
<p>The role combines creative, analytical, and management skills, often requiring flexible hours and travel between sites. This blend of indoor and outdoor work allows landscape architects to see their designs come to life while maintaining high standards of sustainability and functionality.</p>
<h2><b>How to Become a Landscape Architect</b></h2>
<p>Becoming a landscape architect requires a combination of formal education, practical experience, and professional licensing. The first step is earning a <b>bachelor’s or master’s degree in landscape architecture</b> from a program accredited by the Landscape Architecture Accreditation Board (LAAB). Coursework typically covers design principles, horticulture, site analysis, environmental planning, and technical drawing.</p>
<p>After completing their degree, aspiring landscape architects must gain <b>practical experience through internships or entry-level positions</b> under the supervision of licensed professionals. This hands-on experience helps develop skills in project management, design implementation, and client communication.</p>
<p>Licensing is required in all U.S. states, including Colorado. Candidates must pass the <b>Landscape Architect Registration Examination (LARE)</b>, which tests knowledge of design, construction documentation, and professional practice. Many states also require continuing education to maintain licensure.</p>
<p>Strong creativity, technical expertise, problem-solving abilities, and knowledge of environmental and regulatory requirements are essential. By combining education, experience, and licensing, individuals can pursue a rewarding career shaping sustainable and functional outdoor spaces.</p>
<h3><b>1. Earn a Bachelor’s Degree</b></h3>
<p>The first step to becoming a landscape architect is earning a bachelor’s degree in landscape architecture (BLA) from a program accredited by the Landscape Architecture Accreditation Board (LAAB). Accredited programs provide essential training in design principles, site planning, plant science, environmental sustainability, and construction techniques. Students also learn technical skills such as CAD, 3D modeling, and GIS mapping, which are vital for modern landscape design.</p>
<p>During your degree, gaining hands-on experience through studio projects, fieldwork, and internships is highly recommended. This practical exposure helps students understand real-world challenges, including site evaluation, soil analysis, and climate considerations. Completing a strong academic foundation ensures aspiring landscape architects are well-prepared for professional practice and the next steps toward licensure.</p>
<h3><b>2. Gain Experience</b></h3>
<p>Gaining practical experience is a crucial step in becoming a licensed landscape architect. After or during a bachelor’s degree, aspiring professionals typically complete internships or entry-level positions under the supervision of licensed landscape architects. These experiences expose them to real-world projects, including residential, commercial, and public space design.</p>
<p>On-the-job experience helps develop skills in site analysis, project management, plant selection, hardscape installation, and client communication. In Colorado, experience also teaches designers to consider local soil types, water efficiency, and seasonal climate challenges. This hands-on training is essential not only for skill development but also to meet the requirements for the Landscape Architect Registration Examination (LARE). Consistent, supervised experience bridges academic knowledge with practical expertise for a successful career.</p>
<h3><b>3. Consider Getting a Master’s Degree</b></h3>
<p>While a bachelor’s degree is sufficient for many entry-level landscape architecture positions, pursuing a master’s degree (MLA) can open doors to advanced opportunities, specialized fields, and leadership roles. A master’s program allows designers to deepen their knowledge of sustainable design, urban planning, environmental restoration, and complex site analysis.</p>
<p>Graduate programs often focus on research, large-scale projects, and innovative technologies, including advanced CAD, GIS mapping, and 3D modeling techniques. This additional education is particularly valuable for those interested in working on municipal projects, commercial developments, or ecologically sensitive landscapes. In Colorado, a master’s degree can help professionals address regional challenges such as water conservation, elevation changes, and soil variability while enhancing career prospects, earning potential, and professional credibility in the landscape architecture field.</p>
<h3><b>4. Pass the Licensing Exam</b></h3>
<p>Passing the licensing exam is a critical step in becoming a professional landscape architect. In the United States, this involves successfully completing the <b>Landscape Architect Registration Examination (LARE)</b>, which tests knowledge in site design, construction documentation, project management, and professional practice. The exam ensures that candidates have the technical expertise, problem-solving skills, and ethical understanding required to practice safely and effectively.</p>
<p>In Colorado, passing the LARE is mandatory to obtain a landscape architect license and legally offer design services. Preparation often involves a combination of academic knowledge, practical experience, and study guides or prep courses. Successfully completing the licensing exam demonstrates professional competence, allowing landscape architects to take on complex projects, collaborate with other professionals, and provide high-quality, sustainable outdoor design solutions.</p>
<h3><b>5. Take State Exams</b></h3>
<p>After passing the national Landscape Architect Registration Examination (LARE), candidates must also meet their state’s specific licensing requirements. In Colorado, this often includes taking additional state exams or submitting documentation to demonstrate familiarity with local building codes, environmental regulations, and zoning laws. These exams ensure that landscape architects understand regional standards and legal responsibilities, which is essential for designing safe, compliant, and sustainable outdoor spaces.</p>
<p>State licensing requirements vary, so it’s important for aspiring professionals to check Colorado’s licensing board guidelines. Successfully completing state exams grants full licensure, allowing landscape architects to legally practice and offer design services within the state. This step ensures both professional credibility and public safety while enabling architects to take on complex, regulated projects.</p>
<h3><b>6. Apply for Jobs</b></h3>
<p>Once licensed, landscape architects can begin applying for professional positions in a variety of settings, including design firms, municipal planning departments, construction companies, and large commercial or residential projects. Job seekers should prepare a strong portfolio showcasing their design projects, CAD drawings, 3D models, and any real-world experience gained during internships or previous positions.</p>
<p>In Colorado, opportunities may focus on residential landscaping, public parks, sustainable design, or large-scale commercial developments. Networking, attending industry events, and joining professional organizations like the American Society of Landscape Architects (ASLA) can also help secure positions. By applying strategically and highlighting both technical skills and creative expertise, newly licensed landscape architects can launch a rewarding career shaping functional, sustainable, and visually appealing outdoor spaces.</p>

